diff --git a/src/partials/mistakes.html b/src/partials/mistakes.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..36ab050
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/partials/mistakes.html
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+<section class="mistakes section" id="mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-title">
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <p class="t-label eyebrow-g">Common mistakes</p>
+    <h2 class="t-h1 sec-title" id="mistakes-title">The 8 mistakes I look for first</h2>
+    <p class="t-lg m-sub">Teams make them in signup, ID checks, onboarding and B2B websites. Each one has a plain fix.</p>
+    <ul class="m-grid"><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-fintech">Fintech</span><h3>Asking for an ID photo with no guidance</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>People don’t know what counts as a good photo, so they retry. Name or address mismatches only show up after a rejection.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Show an example photo, lighting tips and the matching rules before the camera opens.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-fintech">Fintech</span><h3>Rejecting with “something went wrong”</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>A message like “an issue with your identity verification” and nothing else.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Say what failed, give one next step, and keep the person’s progress.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-fintech">Fintech</span><h3>Leaving no way out</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>Retry loops that end at a support form.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Offer another document, a human chat, or a way to come back later.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-saas">SaaS</span><h3>Mistaking setup for value</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>Profile photos, team invites and an empty dashboard before anything useful happens.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Pick one first win and show it before the setup.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-saas">SaaS</span><h3>One onboarding for everyone, then silence</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>Every new user gets the same tour, and nobody follows up after login.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Ask one question to route people, and prompt them at the moment they stall.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-saas">SaaS</span><h3>Asking for too much before showing value</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>Long forms and forced accounts before anyone has seen what the product does.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Ask only what the next step needs, and let people look around first.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-b2b">B2B</span><h3>A website that tells a different story from sales</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>69% of B2B buyers report that the website and the sellers disagree (Gartner, 2025).</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Keep one story from the first page to the demo call, and offer a smaller first step than a demo.</p></li><li class="m-card"><span class="m-tag m-any">Any product</span><h3>Blaming the page when the problem is who arrives</h3><p class="m-see"><span>What people see</span>Teams redesign a page that is fine while the wrong visitors keep arriving.</p><p class="m-fix"><span>Do this instead</span>Check who is arriving and why before changing screens. A good audit tells you when the answer isn’t design.</p></li></ul>
+    <p class="m-cta"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/start/">Find these in your product</a><span>A 5-day audit shows which ones you have and what to fix first.</span></p>
+  </div>
+</section>
